25
00:01:22.833 --> 00:01:25.966
This is precisely what you did
at the end of the previous video.

26
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:30.866
You can also use a guardrail directly
if you want access to the output

27
00:01:30.866 --> 00:01:34.566
of the guardrail, without lots of layers
of abstraction in the middle.

28
00:01:34.766 --> 00:01:37.300
Again, for debugging purposes.

29
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:39.800
However, if you wrap your guardrail

30
00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.800
inside a guard, you get several benefits.

31
00:01:43.133 --> 00:01:46.533
First of all,
a guard allows you to combine

32
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:50.600
multiple guardrails in a single execution
step.

33
00:01:50.866 --> 00:01:55.033
Typically, for an application,
you wouldn't just want a single guardrail.

34
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:56.500
You'll want to make sure

35
00:01:56.500 --> 00:02:00.933
that your chatbot doesn't hallucinate
and also doesn't use any profanity.

36
00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:02.933
A guard

37
00:02:02.933 --> 00:02:07.733
also allows a lot of benefits, such
as streaming support, where you can allow

38
00:02:07.733 --> 00:02:12.266
the LLM stream its output
and then in real-time, validate it

39
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.500
like chunks as they're streamed out to you
so that your users end up

40
00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.100
getting really fast stream results
that are also protected and validated.

41
00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:26.266
You can also get OpenAI
compatible LLM endpoints with a guard,

42
00:02:26.633 --> 00:02:30.900
so that it's a single line of code
change to go from using OpenAI

43
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.366
or an LLM directly to using a protected
LLM endpoint instead.

44
00:02:36.166 --> 00:02:40.300
You also get out of the box
logging and error handling support

45
00:02:40.466 --> 00:02:45.166
so that every guard execution is logged
and can be analyzed later

46
00:02:45.166 --> 00:02:48.166
to see how your application is performing
overall.

